/* FIE402 · lynlæring — rene data for minispillene i js/bundle-lyn.js.
   Alt innhold på engelsk, siden faget og eksamen er på engelsk.
   Konvensjoner: beta_U vektes UTEN skatt, pre-tax WACC = rU, skatteskjoldet
   diskonteres med rU ved konstant D/E og med rD ved fast permanent gjeld.
   Ingen HTML-tagger: motoren escaper tekst, så symboler skrives som ren tekst
   (rE, rU, V^L, beta_U, tau_c). */
window.EDU_DATA = window.EDU_DATA || {};
window.EDU_DATA.lyn = {

  /* ================= SANT ELLER USANT ================= */
  tf: [
    { s: "Debt creates value for the shareholders because the interest rate on debt is lower than the required return on equity.", answer: false, why: "This is the classic cheap-debt fallacy. Adding debt raises rE exactly enough to offset the cheaper financing: rE = rU + (D/E)(rU - rD), so the weighted average stays at rU. Only a friction such as the tax shield can move value.", ch: 6 },
    { s: "A merger that raises the acquirer's earnings per share creates value for the acquirer's shareholders.", answer: false, why: "EPS accretion is arithmetic, not value. Buying a low P/E target mechanically lifts EPS even with zero synergies, because you add earnings faster than you add shares. Value comes from the NPV of the deal, not from the EPS line.", ch: 26 },
    { s: "Higher volatility of the underlying asset lowers the value of a call option, because more risk is always bad.", answer: false, why: "An option truncates the downside: you only exercise when it pays. More volatility fattens both tails, but the losing tail is capped at zero, so both calls and puts are worth more. This is the single most tested comparative static in the option part.", ch: 23 },
    { s: "The pecking order theory requires that capital markets are inefficient.", answer: false, why: "It requires asymmetric information, not inefficiency. Investors are perfectly rational and price securities correctly given what they can observe; the problem is that managers know more, so an equity issue is read as bad news.", ch: 13 },
    { s: "In a perfect market a leveraged recapitalisation leaves the share price unchanged at the moment the shares are repurchased.", answer: true, why: "The announcement, if it carries news, moves the price. The execution does not: the firm pays out cash worth exactly what it takes on in debt, so each remaining share still owns the same value. Shares repurchased = D/P at the post-announcement price.", ch: 6 },
    { s: "MM Proposition II says the cost of equity rises linearly in the debt-to-equity ratio.", answer: true, why: "rE = rU + (D/E)(rU - rD) is a straight line in D/E with slope (rU - rD). Leverage does not change the risk of the assets, it only concentrates that risk on a smaller equity base.", ch: 6 },
    { s: "The pre-tax WACC equals the unlevered cost of capital rU.", answer: true, why: "Pre-tax WACC = (E/V)rE + (D/V)rD is just the value-weighted required return on all the firm's claims, which is the required return on the assets themselves. That identity is what lets you back rU out of a twin firm.", ch: 4 },
    { s: "In this course we unlever a beta by weighting beta_E and beta_D with a factor (1 - tau_c) on the debt side.", answer: false, why: "The course convention is the plain value-weighted identity beta_U = (E/V)beta_E + (D/V)beta_D, with no tax term. The tax-adjusted Hamada version belongs to a different set of assumptions and will give you the wrong rU here.", ch: 4 },
    { s: "Under a constant debt-to-equity policy the interest tax shield is discounted at rU.", answer: true, why: "If debt is rescaled with firm value, the tax shield inherits the risk of the assets, so it carries the asset discount rate rU. This exact rule has been a multiple-choice item in every paper that had multiple choice.", ch: 18 },
    { s: "When the firm keeps a fixed, permanent amount of debt outstanding, the tax shield should be discounted at rU.", answer: false, why: "Fixed debt means the interest payments are as safe as the debt itself, so they are discounted at rD. That gives PV(TS) = tau_c·rD·D/rD = tau_c·D, the permanent-debt result.", ch: 18 },
    { s: "The WACC method assumes the firm keeps a constant debt-to-value ratio.", answer: true, why: "A single constant discount rate can only be right if the capital structure weights stay constant. If leverage drifts over time you must either recompute the WACC each period or switch to APV.", ch: 17 },
    { s: "The WACC method and the APV method can legitimately give different values for the same firm under the same assumptions.", answer: false, why: "They are two routes to the same number. If they disagree you have made an arithmetic slip or mixed the assumptions, for example discounting the tax shield at rD while using a constant D/V. The examiner runs this check and expects you to run it too.", ch: 20 },
    { s: "With risky debt outstanding, shareholders can prefer a project that lowers total firm value.", answer: true, why: "This is asset substitution. Equity is a call option, so shareholders keep the upside and creditors absorb the downside. A wider spread of outcomes can be worth more to equity even when the mean is lower.", ch: 9 },
    { s: "Debt overhang can make shareholders reject a project with a strictly positive NPV.", answer: true, why: "If the debt is risky, part of the project's payoff repairs the creditors' claim instead of reaching the shareholders who paid for it. When the creditors capture more than the whole NPV, the rational shareholder declines.", ch: 10 },
    { s: "If shareholders reject a positive-NPV project because of debt overhang, it is because creditors would capture more than the entire NPV.", answer: true, why: "Total gain always equals the project's NPV, so the shareholders' loss is exactly the creditors' gain minus the NPV. If creditors gain 15 on a project worth 10, equity loses 5 and refuses to fund it. This decomposition is a standard consistency check.", ch: 10 },
    { s: "In a Myers-Majluf problem it is enough to compute alpha and compare the old shareholders' payoffs; you do not need to check the investors' beliefs.", answer: false, why: "The final step is checking that the beliefs which produced alpha are rational given the behaviour they induce. If only bad firms issue, investors cannot keep believing they face the average firm, and the candidate equilibrium collapses. Solution keys award points for this step alone.", ch: 12 },
    { s: "In the Myers-Majluf setup a firm that knows it is undervalued is happy to issue equity to fund a positive-NPV project.", answer: false, why: "Issuing sells a claim on the true value at a price based on the market's lower estimate. The dilution can cost the old shareholders more than the project's NPV is worth, so the good firm passes and the project is lost.", ch: 12 },
    { s: "Equity in a levered firm is a call option on the firm's assets with a strike equal to the face value of the debt.", answer: true, why: "At maturity shareholders receive max(V - D, 0): they pay off the debt and keep the residual, or walk away. Everything about equity, including why volatility helps it, follows from that payoff.", ch: 24 },
    { s: "Risky debt can be decomposed as risk-free debt minus a put option written on the firm's assets.", answer: true, why: "Creditors are promised D but receive min(V, D) = D - max(D - V, 0). The short put is exactly the default risk they have written, and its value is the source of the credit spread.", ch: 24 },
    { s: "Risk-neutral valuation is valid only if investors are actually risk neutral.", answer: false, why: "The risk-neutral probability is a pricing device, not a belief. It is the probability that makes the underlying earn rf, and it prices the option correctly precisely because the option can be replicated by the underlying and the risk-free asset.", ch: 22 },
    { s: "The true probability of an up move enters the binomial option price.", answer: false, why: "It does not appear anywhere. The replicating portfolio matches the option payoff in both states, so its cost is the option price whatever the odds. Every year some candidates plug in the real probability and lose the whole sub-question.", ch: 22 },
    { s: "Put-call parity states that C = P + S - PV(K).", answer: true, why: "A call plus PV(K) in the bank and a put plus the share have the same payoff at maturity, so they must cost the same today. The exam uses it to back out one price from the other three.", ch: 21 },
    { s: "A project with a positive NPV today can still be worth delaying.", answer: true, why: "Waiting buys information, and the option to invest only when the news is good has value. You invest now only if the NPV today exceeds the value of the option to wait, not merely if it is above zero.", ch: 25 },
    { s: "The option to wait has positive value even when there is no uncertainty about the project's payoff.", answer: false, why: "With nothing to learn, waiting only postpones the cash flows and destroys value. Option value comes from uncertainty plus the right, not the obligation, to act.", ch: 25 },
    { s: "IPO underpricing is a real cost, and it is borne mainly by the issuer's existing owners.", answer: true, why: "Shares are sold below the price the market immediately pays, so the pre-IPO owners hand the difference to the initial subscribers. Money left on the table = shares sold x (first-day close - offer price).", ch: 14 },
    { s: "The winner's curse explanation of underpricing says that underwriters are simply bad at pricing issues.", answer: false, why: "It says uninformed investors get all of the bad issues and only a rationed share of the good ones. To keep them in the market at all, issues must be underpriced on average. The pricing is deliberate, not incompetent.", ch: 14 },
    { s: "A staggered board makes a hostile takeover harder because a bidder cannot replace the whole board at one annual meeting.", answer: true, why: "Only a fraction of directors are up for election each year, so winning control takes at least two proxy contests. It is one of the standard defences, and the exam wants you to say whose interest it serves.", ch: 27 },
    { s: "Poison pills unambiguously benefit target shareholders.", answer: false, why: "They can raise the price by strengthening the board's bargaining hand, but they can also entrench a management that should be replaced. The honest answer names both effects; the governance exercise rewards exactly that balance.", ch: 27 },
    { s: "In a perfect market a shareholder is indifferent between a dividend and a repurchase of the same size.", answer: true, why: "The dividend arrives as cash and the price falls by the same amount; the repurchase leaves the price alone and the shareholder can sell shares to manufacture the same cash. Total wealth is identical either way.", ch: 15 },
    { s: "A share repurchase raises the share price in a perfect market, because there are fewer shares outstanding afterwards.", answer: false, why: "Both the numerator and the denominator fall by the same proportion: the firm pays out cash worth exactly what the retired shares were worth. Price per share is unchanged, which is the whole point of payout irrelevance.", ch: 15 },
    { s: "With corporate taxes and permanent debt, V^L = V^U + tau_c·D.", answer: true, why: "The perpetual tax shield tau_c·rD·D discounted at rD collapses to tau_c·D. Note what this says: the value of the shield does not depend on rD at all, only on the tax rate and the size of the debt.", ch: 7 },
    { s: "The interest tax shield arises because interest reduces the cash the firm has to hand over to its creditors.", answer: false, why: "It arises because interest is deductible from taxable income, so the government receives less. The gain is a transfer from the tax authority to the firm's investors, which is why it is a real friction rather than a reshuffling of claims.", ch: 7 },
    { s: "Expected bankruptcy costs are borne by the shareholders today, through the price investors are willing to pay.", answer: true, why: "Creditors anticipate the losses and demand a higher yield, or pay less for the bonds. Since the shareholders are the residual claimants who sell those bonds, they pay for the distress costs in advance.", ch: 8 },
    { s: "The trade-off theory implies that the optimal capital structure is 100 percent debt whenever the corporate tax rate is positive.", answer: false, why: "That would be the conclusion from the tax shield alone. The trade-off adds expected distress and agency costs, which rise faster than linearly, so the optimum is interior: the point where the marginal tax benefit equals the marginal distress cost.", ch: 8 },
    { s: "The yield to maturity on risky debt is higher than the expected return on that debt.", answer: true, why: "The yield is the promised return if everything is repaid; the expected return subtracts the probability-weighted loss: rD = y - p·L. Using the yield as the cost of debt in a WACC overstates it.", ch: 5 },
    { s: "The credit spread compensates investors purely for systematic risk.", answer: false, why: "The spread mostly covers expected default losses; only what is left after subtracting p·L is a risk premium. Splitting the spread into these two parts is exactly what the credit-risk questions ask for.", ch: 5 },
    { s: "Only systematic risk is priced, because diversifiable risk can be removed for free by holding a portfolio.", answer: true, why: "An investor who is not compensated for avoidable risk simply diversifies it away. This is why beta, not total volatility, sets the cost of capital, and why a firm's own cash-flow variability does not by itself raise its discount rate.", ch: 3 },
    { s: "A firm whose stock has a higher standard deviation must have a higher cost of capital.", answer: false, why: "Standard deviation includes firm-specific noise, which the market does not pay for. A volatile but low-beta firm can have a lower required return than a stable, high-beta one.", ch: 3 },
    { s: "Interest expense never enters free cash flow.", answer: true, why: "FCF is the cash the assets generate before any financing decision, so it must be independent of how the firm is financed. The value of the interest deduction enters through the discount rate in WACC, or as a separate PV(TS) term in APV, never twice.", ch: 2 },
    { s: "Depreciation is subtracted in the free cash flow calculation because it is a cash cost.", answer: false, why: "It is not a cash cost at all. It is deducted before tax to get the tax saving right, and then added straight back: FCF = EBIT(1 - tau_c) + Depreciation - CapEx - change in NWC.", ch: 2 },
    { s: "The NPV rule is more reliable than the IRR rule when a project's cash flows change sign more than once.", answer: true, why: "Multiple sign changes can produce several IRRs or none at all, and the rule also breaks down for mutually exclusive projects of different scale. NPV adds up in money and never has these pathologies.", ch: 1 },
    { s: "Under a constant debt-to-value policy, the debt level must be adjusted downwards in a year when the levered value falls.", answer: true, why: "D_t = d·V_t^L, so debt follows value both up and down. This adjustment step between periods was H2025 Exercise 4(f) and is where most candidates lose points, because they forget to rescale the debt before computing the next tax shield.", ch: 19 },
    { s: "The flow-to-equity method discounts free cash flow to the firm at the cost of equity.", answer: false, why: "FTE discounts free cash flow to equity, after interest and after debt repayments, at rE, and gives the value of equity directly. Discounting the firm's FCF at rE mixes an unlevered cash flow with a levered rate.", ch: 19 },
    { s: "The coinsurance effect in a merger can raise the value of the combined firm's debt at the shareholders' expense.", answer: true, why: "Two imperfectly correlated cash-flow streams under one roof default less often, so the creditors' claim is safer and worth more. Nothing was created; value moved from equity to debt, which is why it is a poor argument for a merger.", ch: 26 },
    { s: "Diversification by itself creates value for the shareholders of an acquiring firm.", answer: false, why: "Shareholders can diversify in their own portfolios at zero cost, so they will not pay a premium for the firm to do it for them. This is one of the motives you should name as not real, especially when synergies are banned as an answer.", ch: 26 },
    { s: "MM Proposition I holds in a perfect market because investors can undo or replicate the firm's leverage on their own account.", answer: true, why: "Homemade leverage is the arbitrage argument: if a levered firm sold at a premium, an investor could borrow personally, buy the unlevered firm and replicate the payoff more cheaply. Identical payoffs must have identical prices.", ch: 6 },
    { s: "If a comparable firm's leverage is small, you may use its equity beta directly as your unlevered beta.", answer: false, why: "Small leverage means a small error, not no error, and the exam always gives enough information to unlever properly. Skipping the step is a marked error even when the number comes out close.", ch: 4 },
    { s: "Debt can reduce the free cash flow problem by committing managers to hand cash back to investors.", answer: true, why: "Interest and principal are contractual, so cash that would otherwise fund empire building leaves the firm. This is the agency benefit of debt and one side of the trade-off against distress and asset-substitution costs.", ch: 11 },
    { s: "Shareholders of a distressed firm may reject a fairly priced, zero-NPV hedge.", answer: true, why: "Hedging shrinks the spread of outcomes, which transfers value from the option-like equity claim to the creditors. Zero NPV for the firm means a strictly negative change in equity value, so shareholders decline.", ch: 9 },
    { s: "Implied volatility is the volatility that makes the Black-Scholes value equal the observed market price of the option.", answer: true, why: "You invert the formula on the one input you cannot observe. It converts a price into the market's forecast of future volatility, which you can then compare with your own estimate.", ch: 23 },
    { s: "If your estimate of the true volatility is below the implied volatility, the option is underpriced and you should buy it.", answer: false, why: "Lower true volatility means a lower fair value than the market is charging, so the option is overpriced: you sell it and hedge the delta. H2025 Exercise 1 asked exactly this, in words and with no computation.", ch: 23 },
    { s: "APV can only be used when the firm's debt is permanent.", answer: false, why: "APV works for any debt policy; what changes is the rate used on the tax shield, rD for a fixed schedule and rU for a policy that rescales with value. Its flexibility is precisely why the exam uses it for irregular debt paths.", ch: 18 },
    { s: "The risk-neutral probability is rho = ((1 + rf)S0 - Sd)/(Su - Sd).", answer: true, why: "It is the weight that makes the expected one-period return on the underlying equal to rf. Once you have it, every derivative on that tree is priced as the discounted risk-neutral expectation of its payoff.", ch: 22 },
    { s: "An increase in the volatility of a firm's assets raises the value of both its equity and its debt.", answer: false, why: "Total firm value is unchanged, so the two must move in opposite directions. Equity, a call, gains; debt, which is short a put, loses. That transfer is the agency cost of debt in one line.", ch: 24 },
    { s: "The announcement of a seasoned equity issue typically pushes the share price down.", answer: true, why: "Investors infer that management considered the shares to be at least fairly valued, probably overvalued. The average announcement effect is negative, and this is the empirical fingerprint of the Myers-Majluf mechanism.", ch: 13 },
    { s: "The examiner expects the WACC value and the APV value to agree, and states the check explicitly in the solution keys.", answer: true, why: "It is one of the five consistency checks: WACC value = APV value, the weighted average of beta_E and beta_D equals beta_U, and creditors' gain plus shareholders' gain equals the project's NPV. Running them out loud earns credit.", ch: 0 },
    { s: "When dividends are taxed more heavily than capital gains at the personal level, dividends and repurchases remain equivalent for the shareholder.", answer: false, why: "The tax wedge breaks the equivalence and favours repurchases, which is the dividend puzzle: firms still pay dividends anyway, and the explanations are signalling, clienteles and the agency benefit of forcing cash out.", ch: 16 },
    { s: "In a full valuation, excess cash is added to the value of operations at the end, or netted against debt to get net debt.", answer: true, why: "The FCF you discounted came from operations only, so non-operating assets must be added separately. Forgetting the excess cash is a classic silent error in the long valuation exercise.", ch: 20 },
  ],

  /* ================= FIREVALG: BEGREPER OG METODEVALG ================= */
  shift: [
    { q: "The firm maintains a constant debt-to-equity ratio. Which rate discounts the interest tax shield?", options: ["rD", "The after-tax WACC", "rU", "The risk-free rate rf"], answer: 2, why: "If debt is continuously rescaled with firm value, the tax shield is as risky as the assets that drive that value, so it is discounted at rU. This exact question has appeared in every paper that had multiple choice.", ch: 18 },
    { q: "The firm has issued a fixed amount of permanent debt. Which rate discounts the interest tax shield?", options: ["rD", "rU", "rE", "The after-tax WACC"], answer: 0, why: "The interest payments are contractually fixed and as safe as the debt itself, so they carry rD. Discounting tau_c·rD·D forever at rD gives the familiar PV(TS) = tau_c·D.", ch: 18 },
    { q: "Which method values the unlevered firm first and then adds the financing side separately?", options: ["WACC", "APV", "FTE", "The dividend discount model"], answer: 1, why: "APV = V^U + PV(TS), where V^U discounts FCF at rU. Keeping the two pieces apart is what lets you handle any debt schedule and what makes the method transparent to a marker.", ch: 18 },
    { q: "The WACC method is only valid under which assumption about the capital structure?", options: ["A constant amount of debt", "Risk-free debt", "No corporate taxes", "A constant debt-to-value ratio"], answer: 3, why: "One fixed discount rate presumes fixed weights. When leverage drifts you must recompute the WACC each year or move to APV, which is exactly the situation the exam constructs to test the choice.", ch: 17 },
    { q: "In the flow-to-equity method, what is discounted and at what rate?", options: ["FCF at the after-tax WACC", "Free cash flow to equity at rE", "FCF at rU", "The tax shield at rD"], answer: 1, why: "FTE takes the cash flow left for shareholders after interest and debt repayments and discounts it at rE, giving the equity value directly rather than the enterprise value.", ch: 19 },
    { q: "The pre-tax WACC, (E/V)rE + (D/V)rD, is equal to:", options: ["The after-tax WACC", "rE", "rU", "rD"], answer: 2, why: "It is the value-weighted required return on all claims, which is the required return on the underlying assets. This identity is the hinge of the twin-firm routine: it is how you extract rU from a levered comparable.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "How is the unlevered beta computed in this course?", options: ["beta_U = (E/V)beta_E + (D/V)beta_D, with no tax adjustment", "beta_U = beta_E/(1 + (1 - tau_c)D/E)", "beta_U = beta_E, since debt has no beta", "beta_U = (E/D)beta_E + (D/E)beta_D"], answer: 0, why: "The course uses the plain value-weighted identity. The tax-adjusted Hamada formula belongs to a different debt-policy assumption and will give a different rU, so mixing them costs points even when the arithmetic is clean.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "If a firm's debt is risk-free, which statement follows?", options: ["rE = rU", "beta_D = 1", "rD = rU", "beta_D = 0 and rD = rf"], answer: 3, why: "Risk-free debt has no systematic risk, so its beta is zero and it earns the risk-free rate. Then beta_U = (E/V)beta_E and rE = rU + (D/E)(rU - rf), the simplest version of MM II.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "You set beta_D = 0 although the firm's debt is actually risky. What is the consequence?", options: ["beta_U is unaffected", "You understate beta_U and therefore rU", "You overstate beta_U and therefore rU", "Only beta_E is affected"], answer: 1, why: "The debt term drops out of beta_U = (E/V)beta_E + (D/V)beta_D, so the weighted average comes out too low, and every downstream number, rU, the relevered rE and the WACC, inherits the error.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "The debt follows a fixed amortisation schedule set in advance. Which approach fits best?", options: ["WACC with constant weights", "FTE with a constant rE", "APV, discounting each year's tax shield at rD", "The perpetuity formula with a constant D/V"], answer: 2, why: "A predetermined schedule means predetermined and low-risk interest payments, so each year's shield is discounted at rD. The WACC method cannot handle it because the leverage ratio changes every year.", ch: 18 },
    { q: "A firm issues debt D and repurchases shares at price P. How many shares does it retire?", options: ["D·P", "D/P", "P/D", "D/(P·N)"], answer: 1, why: "It spends D at the market price P, so it buys D/P shares. The price it must use is the post-announcement price, since any news about the recapitalisation is already in the market before the buyback starts.", ch: 6 },
    { q: "Equity in a levered firm can be described as:", options: ["A call option on firm value with strike equal to the face value of debt", "A put option on firm value", "A forward contract on firm value", "Risk-free debt minus a call"], answer: 0, why: "The payoff is max(V - D, 0). Once you see equity as a call, the comparative statics follow: more asset volatility, more time, and a lower face value all raise the value of equity.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "Risky corporate debt is equivalent to:", options: ["A call on firm assets", "Risk-free debt plus a call on firm assets", "Risk-free debt minus a put on firm assets", "A put plus a call on firm assets"], answer: 2, why: "Creditors receive min(V, D) = D - max(D - V, 0), so they have effectively written a put to the shareholders. The value of that put is the whole of the default component of the credit spread.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "In a one-period binomial tree, the risk-neutral probability is:", options: ["(S0 - Sd)/(Su - Sd)", "(Su - S0)/(Su - Sd)", "(Cu - Cd)/(Su - Sd)", "((1 + rf)S0 - Sd)/(Su - Sd)"], answer: 3, why: "It is the weight that makes the underlying earn exactly rf. Real probabilities never enter, because the option is priced by replication and the replicating portfolio pays off correctly in both states.", ch: 22 },
    { q: "The number of shares in the replicating portfolio for an option is:", options: ["(Cu - Cd)/(Su - Sd)", "(Su - Sd)/(Cu - Cd)", "(Cu + Cd)/2", "rho·Cu + (1 - rho)·Cd"], answer: 0, why: "Delta is the ratio of the spread of option payoffs to the spread of stock payoffs. With B = (Cd - Sd·Delta)/(1 + rf), the option price is C = S·Delta + B, which must match the risk-neutral answer.", ch: 22 },
    { q: "Put-call parity for European options on a non-dividend-paying stock is:", options: ["C = P - S + PV(K)", "C = P + S - PV(K)", "P = C + S - PV(K)", "C = P + PV(K) - S"], answer: 1, why: "A call plus PV(K) invested at the risk-free rate replicates a put plus the share. The exam typically gives three of the four quantities and asks for the fourth, or uses parity to show two quoted prices are inconsistent.", ch: 21 },
    { q: "The value of the option to wait one year before investing equals:", options: ["The expected NPV next year, discounted", "The largest possible NPV, discounted", "The discounted expected value of max(0, NPV next year) minus max(0, NPV today)", "NPV today minus the expected NPV next year"], answer: 2, why: "Waiting means you invest only in the good states, hence the max(0, ...) inside the expectation, and you give up the NPV you could have taken today. The subtraction is the step candidates most often forget.", ch: 25 },
    { q: "The value of perfect information about a project is:", options: ["E[max(0, NPV_i)] - max(0, E[NPV])", "max(0, E[NPV]) - E[max(0, NPV_i)]", "E[NPV] discounted one year", "The best NPV minus the worst NPV"], answer: 0, why: "With information you can decline the bad states; without it you must commit on the average. The difference is what the information is worth, and it is zero exactly when you would invest, or refuse, in every state anyway.", ch: 25 },
    { q: "In the Myers-Majluf template, the fraction of the firm sold to new investors is:", options: ["I/V_true", "(I + NPV)/E[V]", "I/(V_true + NPV)", "I/(E[V given investors' beliefs] + NPV)"], answer: 3, why: "The price is set on what investors believe, not on the truth, which is why the true type's payoff (1 - alpha)(V_true + payoff) can fall short of simply doing nothing. The final step is checking those beliefs are rational.", ch: 12 },
    { q: "The pecking order predicts that firms finance investment in which order?", options: ["Equity, then debt, then internal funds", "Internal funds, then debt, then equity", "Debt, then equity, then internal funds", "Internal funds, then equity, then debt"], answer: 1, why: "Each step up the ladder is more information-sensitive and therefore more expensive when managers know more than the market. Retained earnings avoid the problem entirely; equity is worst.", ch: 13 },
    { q: "The pecking order theory rests on which assumption?", options: ["Investors are irrational", "Bankruptcy costs are large", "Managers are better informed than investors", "Personal taxes favour debt"], answer: 2, why: "It is an asymmetric-information story, fully compatible with efficient markets and rational pricing. Investors discount an equity issue precisely because they correctly infer what it implies about the issuer.", ch: 13 },
    { q: "H2024 banned synergies as an answer. Which is still a defensible motive for an acquisition?", options: ["Cost synergies in production", "Revenue synergies from cross-selling", "Economies of scale in procurement", "Disciplining an underperforming management team"], answer: 3, why: "The market for corporate control is a governance mechanism: a bidder who can run the assets better bids for them. Undervaluation, tax motives, market power, empire building and hubris are the other names to have ready.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "The maximum exchange ratio an acquirer can offer is the one that:", options: ["Leaves the acquirer's shareholders exactly as well off as without the deal", "Gives the target shareholders zero gain", "Equalises the two firms' earnings per share", "Maximises the post-merger share price"], answer: 0, why: "Beyond that ratio the acquirer hands over more than the entire synergy and destroys value for its own owners. At the bound the post-merger price equals the acquirer's pre-deal price, which is a quick way to check the number.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "The market's implied probability that an announced deal closes is:", options: ["The target's price divided by the offer price", "(Offer price - current price)/Offer price", "(Current price - standalone price)/(Offer price - standalone price)", "(Offer price - standalone price)/Current price"], answer: 2, why: "The target trades at a probability-weighted average of the offer price if the deal closes and the standalone price if it fails. Solving that average for the probability is H2025 Exercise 6.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "Under a constant debt-to-value policy, the debt outstanding in year t is:", options: ["D_t = D_0 for every t", "D_t = d·V_t^L", "D_t = d·FCF_t", "D_t = d·V_t^U"], answer: 1, why: "Debt is a fixed fraction of the levered value, which is what creates the circularity the WACC method resolves for you. It also means debt must be adjusted down when value falls, the step H2025 Exercise 4(f) tested.", ch: 19 },
    { q: "The expected return on risky debt is:", options: ["rD = y", "rD = y + p·L", "rD = rf + p", "rD = y - p·L"], answer: 3, why: "From the promised yield you subtract the probability of default times the loss given default. Using the promised yield as the cost of debt overstates rD, and through the WACC understates the firm's value.", ch: 5 },
    { q: "The credit spread on a corporate bond is:", options: ["The promised yield minus the risk-free rate", "The expected return minus the risk-free rate", "The probability of default", "The loss given default"], answer: 0, why: "It is defined on promised yields. Only the part left after subtracting expected default losses is a genuine risk premium, and separating the two is the standard credit-risk sub-question.", ch: 5 },
    { q: "The effective tax advantage of debt once personal taxes are included is:", options: ["tau_c", "1 - tau_c", "1 - (1 - tau_c)(1 - tau_e)/(1 - tau_i)", "(1 - tau_c)(1 - tau_e)"], answer: 2, why: "Interest is taxed as personal income at tau_i, while equity income first pays corporate tax and then tau_e. If interest is taxed heavily enough at the personal level, the advantage shrinks to zero or turns negative.", ch: 8 },
    { q: "The most widely used explanation of IPO underpricing is:", options: ["Underwriters are poor at valuation", "The winner's curse facing uninformed investors", "A legal cap on the offer price", "Tax rules that favour a low offer price"], answer: 1, why: "Uninformed investors get filled on every overpriced issue and rationed on the good ones, so their unconditional return would be negative at a fair offer price. Underpricing is the price of keeping them in the book.", ch: 14 },
    { q: "Which consistency check does the solution key expect you to perform?", options: ["The IRR equals the WACC", "beta_E equals beta_U", "rE equals rU", "The value from WACC equals the value from APV"], answer: 3, why: "The five checks are: WACC value = APV value, weighted beta_E and beta_D = beta_U, creditors' plus shareholders' gain = the project NPV, creditors' gain = shareholders' loss when total value is fixed, and pre-tax WACC = rU.", ch: 0 },
    { q: "Asset substitution (risk shifting) describes shareholders who:", options: ["Choose a riskier project with lower total value because debt is risky", "Reject a positive-NPV project because creditors capture the gain", "Pay out too much cash as dividends before default", "Refuse to renegotiate the face value of debt"], answer: 0, why: "Equity is a call, so a wider distribution can raise its value even when the mean falls. Naming the mechanism, not just computing the payoffs, is what the key rewards.", ch: 9 },
    { q: "Debt overhang describes shareholders who:", options: ["Choose an excessively risky project", "Issue equity to repay debt early", "Reject a positive-NPV project because creditors would capture more than the NPV", "Hedge a risk they should have kept"], answer: 2, why: "The investment repairs the creditors' claim before it reaches equity. Renegotiating the face value can make both sides better off, and the total gain from doing so must equal the project's NPV.", ch: 10 },
    { q: "Which of these is NOT printed on the exam formula sheet and must be memorised?", options: ["The CAPM", "Which rate discounts the tax shield", "The growing perpetuity formula", "The free cash flow definition"], answer: 1, why: "The sheet gives CAPM, MM II, the beta weighting, both WACC forms, binomial replication and the M&A swap formulas. V^L = V^U + PV(TS), the discount-rate rule, D_t = d·V_t^L, put-call parity and everything on real options are not on it.", ch: 0 },
    { q: "Your estimate of true volatility is below the option's implied volatility. The option is:", options: ["Underpriced, so you buy it", "Fairly priced", "Impossible to value without N(d) tables", "Overpriced, so you sell it and hedge the delta"], answer: 3, why: "Value rises with volatility, so a market pricing in more volatility than you believe is charging too much. H2025 Exercise 1 asked this in words, with no computation, precisely because the tables were dropped from the sheet.", ch: 23 },
    { q: "What is the correct order of the twin-firm routine?", options: ["Comparable rE, then beta_E via CAPM, then beta_U by weighting, then rU via CAPM, then relever to your own D/E, then rE, then WACC", "Comparable rE, then rU, then WACC, then beta_U", "Your own beta_E, then beta_U, then the comparable's rU", "Comparable WACC, then rU, then beta_U, then rE"], answer: 0, why: "You must strip the comparable's leverage before you can apply your own. The one place candidates go wrong is reusing the comparable's D/E when relevering instead of their own.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "The relation beta_E = N(d1)(V/E)beta_U tells you that:", options: ["Debt has no systematic risk", "Equity and asset betas are equal", "Equity beta exceeds asset beta because equity is a levered, option-like claim on the assets", "Equity beta falls as leverage rises"], answer: 2, why: "N(d1)·V/E exceeds one for a levered firm, so equity amplifies asset risk, and the amplification grows as the firm approaches distress. It is the option-pricing version of the MM II leverage effect.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "The firm keeps a constant D/V and you already have rE, rD, the weights and tau_c. Which method is quickest?", options: ["APV", "WACC", "FTE", "A binomial tree"], answer: 1, why: "Everything the after-tax WACC needs is already in hand and the constant-ratio assumption is satisfied, so a single discount rate does the job. Switch to APV when the debt path is irregular or when the exam asks for both.", ch: 17 },
    { q: "A shareholder with a 5 to 10 percent stake believes the CEO is shirking. Which action is realistically available?", options: ["Dismissing the CEO directly", "Setting executive pay unilaterally", "Ordering an immediate liquidation of the firm", "Running a proxy fight to replace board members"], answer: 3, why: "A blockholder acts through the board, not around it: proxy contests, shareholder proposals, say-on-pay votes, public engagement, private negotiation, selling the stake (the Wall Street walk) or inviting a bidder. H2025 Exercise 2 wanted three such actions with a sentence of reasoning each.", ch: 27 },
    { q: "When is APV strictly more convenient than the WACC method?", options: ["When leverage changes over time in a known way", "When the firm has no debt", "When the firm has a constant debt-to-value ratio", "When the tax rate is zero"], answer: 0, why: "A known but changing debt path breaks the constant-weight assumption behind a single WACC, while APV simply discounts each year's tax shield at the right rate. With no debt or no taxes the two methods coincide.", ch: 18 },
    { q: "In MM without taxes, as the firm increases leverage the WACC:", options: ["Falls, because debt is cheaper", "Stays constant at rU", "Rises, because equity gets riskier", "First falls, then rises"], answer: 1, why: "The cheaper debt and the rising rE cancel exactly, which is Proposition II restated. Any answer that has the WACC moving in a perfect market is really claiming that capital structure creates value out of nothing.", ch: 6 },
  ],

  /* ================= MEKANISME-KJEDER ================= */
  chains: [
    { title: "Debt overhang: why shareholders turn down a good project", steps: ["The firm has risky debt outstanding, so default is possible in the bad state", "A positive-NPV project appears, and only the shareholders can fund it", "Much of the payoff arrives in states where the debt is not being repaid in full", "Creditors capture more of the gain than the entire NPV of the project", "Shareholders end up worse off and rationally decline the project"], why: "Name the mechanism and then verify it: the creditors' gain minus the shareholders' loss must equal the project's NPV. Renegotiating the face value can make both sides better off, which is why covenants and short maturities exist.", ch: 10 },
    { title: "Asset substitution: why the risky project wins", steps: ["The firm is levered and the debt is risky", "Equity is a call on firm value, so the shareholders' downside is capped at zero", "A riskier project widens the distribution of firm value", "The extra upside goes to equity while the extra downside lands on the creditors", "Shareholders choose the risky project even though total firm value falls"], why: "The agency cost is the first-best value minus the value actually chosen. Because it is anticipated, the creditors charge for it up front, so the shareholders pay for their own future misbehaviour.", ch: 9 },
    { title: "Why the cost of equity rises with leverage", steps: ["The risk of the assets, beta_U, is set by what the firm does, not how it is financed", "Debt takes the safest slice of the cash flows first", "The same asset risk now rests on a smaller equity base", "beta_E = beta_U + (D/E)(beta_U - beta_D) grows linearly in D/E", "rE = rU + (D/E)(rU - rD), while the pre-tax WACC stays at rU"], why: "This is MM II and the reason cheap debt is an illusion in a perfect market. The check to run afterwards is that the weighted average of beta_E and beta_D takes you back to beta_U.", ch: 6 },
    { title: "How the interest tax shield raises firm value", steps: ["Interest is deductible, so a levered firm reports lower taxable income", "The tax bill falls by tau_c times the interest paid each year", "That saving is a cash flow to investors which the unlevered firm never receives", "Discounting the stream gives PV(TS); with permanent debt it collapses to tau_c·D", "V^L = V^U + PV(TS), and the share price jumps at the announcement"], why: "The gain is a transfer from the tax authority, and in a recapitalisation the existing shareholders capture it, because the price rises before any shares are repurchased.", ch: 7 },
    { title: "IPO underpricing and the winner's curse", steps: ["Some investors are informed about the issue's true value and others are not", "Uninformed investors receive their full allocation in the overpriced deals", "In the underpriced deals informed demand crowds them out and they are rationed", "Their return conditional on getting shares is worse than the average return", "The offer price must sit below fair value to keep them bidding at all"], why: "Underpricing is deliberate, not incompetence, and the cost falls on the pre-IPO owners: money left on the table = shares sold x (first-day close - offer price).", ch: 14 },
    { title: "How an exit option creates value", steps: ["A project you can never leave is valued as a perpetuity of its cash flow", "With the right to abandon, you continue only while continuing beats the alternative", "In bad states the perpetuity is replaced by a short annuity plus any salvage value", "Truncating the bad tail raises the project's value today", "The difference between the two valuations is the value of the exit option"], why: "Almost every real-option question is this comparison: value the rigid plan, value the flexible plan, and report the difference. The indifference point is the salvage value at which the two are equal.", ch: 25 },
    { title: "A leveraged recapitalisation, step by step", steps: ["The firm announces it will issue debt D and repurchase shares", "Any value effect, such as the tax shield, enters the share price at the announcement", "The firm issues the debt and buys D/P shares at the post-announcement price P", "Equity value and cash both fall by D, so the price per share is unchanged", "Leverage is higher, so rE rises, while the pre-tax WACC stays at rU"], why: "Keeping announcement and execution apart is what the 18-point recapitalisation exercise is really testing. A shareholder who sells into the buyback and one who holds must end up equally well off.", ch: 6 },
    { title: "The twin-firm routine", steps: ["Take the comparable's equity beta, or derive it from its rE with the CAPM", "Weight beta_E and beta_D with the comparable's E/V and D/V to get beta_U", "Put beta_U back into the CAPM to get rU", "Relever with your own D/E: rE = rU + (D/E)(rU - rD)", "Compute the after-tax WACC using your own weights and tax rate"], why: "This appears in all eleven past papers. The classic slip is relevering with the comparable's D/E instead of your own, which quietly corrupts every number that follows.", ch: 4 },
    { title: "Myers-Majluf: why the good firm stays silent", steps: ["Managers know the true value while investors know only the distribution", "The issue price therefore reflects the average firm, not the good one", "Selling a fraction alpha of an undervalued firm hands value to the new investors", "For the good firm that transfer is larger than the project's NPV", "The good firm does not issue, which confirms investors' pessimistic beliefs"], why: "The last step is the one that earns points: you must show the beliefs behind alpha are rational given the behaviour they produce. The outcome is inefficient even though nobody is irrational.", ch: 12 },
    { title: "Valuing a levered firm with the WACC method", steps: ["Build FCF from EBIT: add depreciation, subtract CapEx and the change in NWC", "Get rU from a comparable and relever to the firm's own target D/E", "Compute the after-tax WACC with the target weights", "Discount every free cash flow, including the terminal value, at that WACC", "Add non-operating items such as excess cash to reach the value of the firm"], why: "The method is valid only under a constant debt-to-value ratio. If the exam gives you a debt path instead of a ratio, this route is the wrong one and APV is the right one.", ch: 17 },
    { title: "Valuing the same firm with APV", steps: ["Discount the same free cash flows at rU to get the unlevered value V^U", "Set the debt level from the stated policy: a fixed schedule, or D_t = d·V_t^L", "Compute each year's tax shield as tau_c·rD·D", "Discount the shields at rD for fixed debt, or at rU under a constant ratio", "Add them up: V^L = V^U + PV(TS), and check it matches the WACC value"], why: "Doing the same case both ways is the biggest single exercise in most papers. The agreement of the two answers is a stated consistency check, so write it down explicitly.", ch: 18 },
    { title: "From asset volatility to a wider credit spread", steps: ["Equity is a call on firm value; risky debt is risk-free debt minus a put", "Higher asset volatility raises the value of both options", "The call gains, so equity is worth more, while the written put costs more, so the debt is worth less", "The promised yield rises above rf and the credit spread widens"], why: "Firm value has not changed, so this is a pure transfer from creditors to shareholders. It is asset substitution expressed in option language, and it explains why lenders write covenants.", ch: 24 },
    { title: "The trade-off theory optimum", steps: ["Start unlevered and add debt: each krone of debt brings a tax shield", "Expected distress costs rise faster than linearly, and agency costs of debt come on top once the debt is risky", "The optimum is where the marginal tax benefit equals the marginal distress and agency cost", "Past that point additional debt destroys value"], why: "This is the answer to why we do not see all-debt firms despite tau_c·D. It also predicts the cross-section: safe, tangible-asset firms borrow more than volatile, intangible ones.", ch: 8 },
    { title: "The agency benefit of debt", steps: ["The firm produces free cash flow that management controls", "With no claim on that cash, managers can fund empire-building projects with negative NPV", "Debt service is contractual: it removes the cash and forces managers back to the capital market to be screened", "Investment discipline improves and firm value rises"], why: "This is the offsetting benefit in the trade-off, and the logic behind leveraged buyouts as a governance mechanism. It is strongest in mature firms with lots of cash and few good projects.", ch: 11 },
    { title: "Stock-swap arithmetic in an acquisition", steps: ["Fix the exchange ratio ER: the target's shareholders receive x = ER x N_T new shares", "The combined value is A + T + S, the two stand-alone values plus the synergies", "The post-merger share price is P_new = (A + T + S)/(N_A + x)", "The target's gain is x·P_new - T; the acquirer's gain is N_A·P_new - A", "The two gains must add up to S, which is the check to run"], why: "The maximum exchange ratio is the one that drives the acquirer's gain to zero, which happens exactly when P_new equals the acquirer's pre-deal price. Distinguish the offered premium from the actual premium.", ch: 26 },
    { title: "Backing the deal probability out of market prices", steps: ["Before the bid the target trades at its stand-alone value, and the bid puts a higher price on the table if the deal closes", "After the announcement the target trades somewhere between the two", "That price is the probability-weighted average of the two outcomes", "Solving for p gives the market's implied probability that the deal completes"], why: "H2025 Exercise 6 asked for exactly this, and the same logic prices the long-target short-acquirer arbitrage position. State the two outcomes explicitly before you solve.", ch: 26 },
    { title: "The option to wait", steps: ["The project has a positive NPV today, so the naive rule says invest now", "Waiting one year reveals the state, and in the bad state you simply do not invest, so the payoff is floored at zero", "The value of waiting is the discounted expectation of max(0, NPV) next year", "Invest today only if today's NPV exceeds the value of waiting"], why: "A positive NPV is necessary but not sufficient. The value of the option comes from uncertainty plus the right, not the obligation, to invest, so it vanishes when there is nothing to learn.", ch: 25 },
    { title: "Adjusting the debt between periods", steps: ["The stated policy is a constant debt-to-value ratio d", "Recompute the levered continuation value V_t^L at the end of the period", "Set the new debt level D_t = d·V_t^L, which is smaller if value has fallen, and repay the difference", "Next year's tax shield is computed on the new, smaller debt"], why: "This was H2025 Exercise 4(f) and is where most candidates lose points: they keep the old debt level and overstate every later tax shield.", ch: 19 },
    { title: "Coinsurance in a merger", steps: ["Two firms each have risky debt outstanding, and their cash flows are less than perfectly correlated", "Merging pools the cash flows, so a shortfall in one can be covered by the other and default becomes less likely", "The combined debt is worth more, and nothing was created: the creditors' gain comes out of the shareholders' pockets"], why: "State-by-state it is a pure transfer, which is why diversification is a weak merger motive. It is the mirror image of asset substitution: less risk helps creditors, more risk helps shareholders.", ch: 26 },
    { title: "How a blockholder disciplines a shirking CEO", steps: ["A 5 to 10 percent stake makes monitoring worth its cost, unlike a tiny holding", "The first move is private engagement with the board, demanding a change", "If that fails: a shareholder proposal, a public campaign or a proxy fight for board seats", "Governance changes follow: independent directors, committees, performance-linked pay", "As a last resort, selling the stake depresses the price and invites a disciplinary takeover"], why: "H2025 Exercise 2 wanted three such actions at four points each, with two or three sentences of reasoning per action. Escalation from cheap to costly is a good way to structure the answer.", ch: 27 },
  ],

  /* ================= OPP ELLER NED (komparativ statikk) ================= */
  updown: [
    { q: "The volatility of the underlying asset rises. What happens to the value of a call option?", dir: "opp", why: "The payoff is truncated at zero, so a wider distribution adds to the upside without adding to the downside. Higher volatility is good for the holder of any option, call or put.", ch: 23 },
    { q: "The volatility of the underlying asset rises. What happens to the value of a put option?", dir: "opp", why: "Same logic as for the call: the put pays max(K - S, 0), so a fatter left tail helps while the fatter right tail costs nothing extra. Volatility raises both option prices, which is why parity still holds.", ch: 23 },
    { q: "The volatility of a levered firm's assets rises. What happens to the value of its equity?", dir: "opp", why: "Equity is a call on firm value with strike equal to the face value of the debt, so it gains from volatility. This is why shareholders of a distressed firm are tempted into risk shifting.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "The volatility of a levered firm's assets rises. What happens to the market value of its risky debt?", dir: "ned", why: "Firm value is unchanged and equity gains, so debt must lose. In option terms the creditors are short a put, and that put has just become more valuable.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "The volatility of a firm's assets rises. What happens to the credit spread on its debt?", dir: "opp", why: "The debt is worth less today while the promised repayment is unchanged, so the promised yield rises above rf by more than before. Default risk, not systematic risk, drives most of the widening.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "A firm increases its debt-to-equity ratio in a perfect market. What happens to rE?", dir: "opp", why: "rE = rU + (D/E)(rU - rD) rises linearly in D/E. The asset risk has not changed; it is simply concentrated on a thinner equity slice.", ch: 6 },
    { q: "A firm increases its debt-to-equity ratio. What happens to beta_E?", dir: "opp", why: "beta_E = beta_U + (D/E)(beta_U - beta_D). Leverage amplifies the systematic risk borne by the equity, which is exactly the same statement as MM II expressed in betas.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "A firm increases leverage in a world with corporate taxes only. What happens to the after-tax WACC?", dir: "ned", why: "rwacc = rU - d·tau_c·rD: the tax shield subtracts from the unlevered rate in proportion to the debt ratio. Without taxes the WACC would be flat at rU.", ch: 17 },
    { q: "The corporate tax rate falls. What happens to the present value of the interest tax shield?", dir: "ned", why: "PV(TS) is proportional to tau_c, and with permanent debt it is exactly tau_c·D. A tax cut therefore reduces the value of leverage, other things equal.", ch: 7 },
    { q: "The corporate tax rate rises. What happens to the optimal leverage under the trade-off theory?", dir: "opp", why: "A larger tax shield pushes the marginal benefit curve up, so the point where it meets the marginal distress and agency cost moves to the right. Higher-tax environments should show more debt.", ch: 8 },
    { q: "Expected costs of financial distress increase. What happens to the optimal debt level?", dir: "ned", why: "The marginal cost curve shifts up, so it intersects the constant marginal tax benefit at a lower level of debt. This predicts that volatile, intangible-asset firms borrow less.", ch: 8 },
    { q: "The risk-free rate rises. What happens to the value of a European call option?", dir: "opp", why: "The strike is paid in the future, so a higher discount rate shrinks PV(K). Parity, C = P + S - PV(K), makes the direction immediate.", ch: 21 },
    { q: "The risk-free rate rises. What happens to the value of a European put option?", dir: "ned", why: "The put holder receives K in the future, and that promise is worth less when discounted harder. It is the mirror image of the effect on the call.", ch: 21 },
    { q: "The strike price of a call option is increased. What happens to the call's value?", dir: "ned", why: "You must pay more for the same asset, so every payoff max(S - K, 0) is weakly smaller. For equity as a call, the strike is the face value of the debt, so more debt means less valuable equity.", ch: 21 },
    { q: "The strike price of a put option is increased. What happens to the put's value?", dir: "opp", why: "The put holder sells at K, so a higher strike is a better deal in every state. The same higher strike that hurts the call helps the put.", ch: 21 },
    { q: "The time to maturity of an American call option is extended. What happens to its value?", dir: "opp", why: "A longer horizon gives more time for the underlying to move and lets you postpone paying the strike. More rights, no extra obligations, so the value can only rise.", ch: 23 },
    { q: "A firm pays an unexpectedly large dividend. What happens to the value of a call option on its stock?", dir: "ned", why: "The share price drops by roughly the dividend on the ex-day, and the call holder receives no compensation for it. Cash leaving the underlying is bad news for calls and good news for puts.", ch: 21 },
    { q: "A levered firm switches to a riskier project with the same expected value. What happens to the value of the outstanding debt?", dir: "ned", why: "The creditors' short put becomes more valuable, so their claim is worth less. This is asset substitution, and the loss to creditors is exactly the shareholders' gain.", ch: 9 },
    { q: "The face value of a firm's risky debt is increased. What happens to shareholders' willingness to fund a positive-NPV project?", dir: "ned", why: "The bigger the claim standing ahead of them, the more of the new project's payoff goes to repairing the debt. This is debt overhang, and it is why highly levered firms underinvest.", ch: 10 },
    { q: "Creditors agree to reduce the face value of the debt as part of a renegotiation. What happens to the value of the equity?", dir: "opp", why: "A lower strike makes the shareholders' call more valuable, and it restores their incentive to invest. Both sides can gain: the total gain from the restored project equals its NPV.", ch: 10 },
    { q: "A firm announces a permanent debt-financed share repurchase and the only friction is corporate taxes. What happens to the share price at the announcement?", dir: "opp", why: "The market capitalises the new tax shield immediately: the price rises by tau_c·D divided by the number of shares before any repurchase takes place. Existing shareholders capture the gain.", ch: 7 },
    { q: "A firm announces a seasoned equity issue. What typically happens to its share price?", dir: "ned", why: "Investors infer that managers do not consider the shares undervalued. The negative average announcement return is the empirical signature of the Myers-Majluf mechanism.", ch: 13 },
    { q: "IPO underpricing increases. What happens to the proceeds received by the issuing owners?", dir: "ned", why: "They sell the same shares at a lower price, and the difference is captured by the initial subscribers. Money left on the table = shares sold x (first-day close - offer price).", ch: 14 },
    { q: "A share goes ex-dividend in a perfect market. What happens to the share price?", dir: "ned", why: "The buyer no longer receives the dividend, so the price falls by the amount of the dividend. Total shareholder wealth is unchanged: cash replaces share value one for one.", ch: 15 },
    { q: "A firm completes an open-market share repurchase. What happens to the number of shares outstanding?", dir: "ned", why: "Shares are retired: with a repurchase of D at price P, exactly D/P shares disappear. The price per share, by contrast, is unchanged in a perfect market.", ch: 15 },
    { q: "An acquirer buys a target with a lower price-earnings ratio in a stock swap with zero synergies. What happens to the acquirer's EPS?", dir: "opp", why: "You add the target's earnings faster than you add shares, so EPS rises mechanically. Nothing has been created, which is why EPS accretion is never an argument for a deal.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "The synergies expected from a takeover increase. What happens to the maximum exchange ratio the acquirer can offer?", dir: "opp", why: "The bound is set by the point where the acquirer's shareholders are exactly as well off as without the deal, so a larger pie lets you give away more shares. Check it by verifying that P_new equals the acquirer's pre-deal price at the bound.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "The market becomes more confident that an announced takeover will close. What happens to the target's share price?", dir: "opp", why: "The price is the probability-weighted average of the offer price and the stand-alone price. More weight on the higher outcome pushes the price towards the offer.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "Uncertainty about next year's project value increases. What happens to the value of the option to wait?", dir: "opp", why: "You will invest only in the good states, so a wider spread raises the expectation of max(0, NPV) while the bad states stay floored at zero. No uncertainty means no reason to wait.", ch: 25 },
    { q: "The salvage value available on abandonment rises. What happens to the value of a project that includes an exit option?", dir: "opp", why: "The floor under the bad states is lifted, so the truncated distribution improves. The indifference point is where the salvage value exactly equals the present value of continuing.", ch: 25 },
    { q: "The growth rate g increases, with g still below the WACC. What happens to the terminal value FCF/(rwacc - g)?", dir: "opp", why: "The denominator shrinks, so the value rises, and it does so steeply when g is close to rwacc. That sensitivity is why the exam likes to solve backwards for the g implied by a given value.", ch: 17 },
    { q: "The after-tax WACC rises. What happens to the value of the firm's operations?", dir: "ned", why: "Every future cash flow is discounted more heavily. A one percentage point error in the WACC can move a terminal value by double digits, which is why the twin-firm step deserves care.", ch: 17 },
    { q: "The unlevered beta beta_U is revised upwards. What happens to rU?", dir: "opp", why: "rU = rf + beta_U x the market risk premium, so the asset discount rate follows the asset beta directly. Everything downstream, rE and the WACC, moves with it.", ch: 3 },
    { q: "The risk-free rate rises while beta and the market risk premium are unchanged. What happens to the required return on equity?", dir: "opp", why: "The CAPM line shifts up in parallel: rE = rf + beta_E x MRP. The premium for risk is unchanged, but the base you add it to is higher.", ch: 3 },
    { q: "You revise the debt beta upwards, holding beta_U and D/E fixed. What happens to beta_E?", dir: "ned", why: "beta_E = beta_U + (D/E)(beta_U - beta_D): a larger beta_D shrinks the gap being levered up. If the debt carries more of the systematic risk, less of it is left for the equity.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "The personal tax rate on interest income rises. What happens to the effective tax advantage of debt?", dir: "ned", why: "tau* = 1 - (1 - tau_c)(1 - tau_e)/(1 - tau_i) falls as tau_i rises. Investors demand a higher pre-tax yield to compensate, which eats into the corporate-level saving.", ch: 8 },
    { q: "Asymmetric information between managers and investors becomes more severe. What happens to the cost of raising equity?", dir: "opp", why: "Investors discount more heavily for the risk of buying an overvalued issue, so the adverse-selection discount grows. That is what pushes firms up the pecking order towards internal funds and debt.", ch: 12 },
    { q: "A firm accumulates more internal funds. Under the pecking order, what happens to the likelihood that it issues equity?", dir: "ned", why: "Retained earnings carry no information cost, so they are used first and equity is a last resort. This is why profitable firms tend to be less levered, contrary to the naive tax-shield prediction.", ch: 13 },
    { q: "Capital expenditure rises with everything else unchanged. What happens to free cash flow?", dir: "ned", why: "CapEx is subtracted in full: FCF = EBIT(1 - tau_c) + Depreciation - CapEx - change in NWC. Only the depreciation of that investment comes back, and only later.", ch: 2 },
    { q: "Net working capital increases with everything else unchanged. What happens to free cash flow?", dir: "ned", why: "Cash is tied up in receivables and inventory, so the change in NWC is subtracted. The reverse also holds: running down inventory releases cash and raises FCF.", ch: 2 },
    { q: "Depreciation rises while EBITDA, CapEx and NWC are unchanged, and the tax rate is positive. What happens to free cash flow?", dir: "opp", why: "FCF = EBITDA(1 - tau_c) + tau_c x Depreciation, so the only effect left is the depreciation tax shield. Depreciation reduces the tax bill without ever leaving the firm as cash.", ch: 2 },
    { q: "The probability of default rises while the promised yield is unchanged. What happens to the expected return on the debt?", dir: "ned", why: "rD = y - p·L: the promised yield now buys a smaller expected payment. Using y instead of rD in a WACC overstates the cost of debt and understates the firm's value.", ch: 5 },
    { q: "A target firm adopts a poison pill. What happens to the probability that a hostile takeover succeeds?", dir: "ned", why: "The pill lets other shareholders buy discounted shares if a bidder crosses a threshold, making the acquisition prohibitively dilutive. Whether that serves shareholders or entrenches management is exactly the question the governance exercise wants discussed.", ch: 27 },
    { q: "A mature, cash-rich firm takes on debt. What happens to managerial empire building?", dir: "ned", why: "Contractual interest and principal remove the discretionary cash and force managers back to the capital market for funding. This is the agency benefit of debt and the logic behind the leveraged buyout.", ch: 11 },
    { q: "Under a constant debt-to-value policy, the levered value of the firm rises. What happens to next year's interest tax shield?", dir: "opp", why: "D_t = d·V_t^L, so the debt is rescaled upwards and tau_c·rD·D grows with it. The same mechanism forces the debt down, and the shield with it, when value falls.", ch: 19 },
    { q: "An out-of-the-money option approaches its expiry date with the underlying unchanged. What happens to its time value?", dir: "ned", why: "There is less time left for the underlying to move into the money, so the time value decays towards zero. At expiry only the intrinsic value survives.", ch: 23 },
  ],

  /* ================= HODEREGNING (kalkulator tillatt: gjenkjenn formelen) ================= */
  hode: [
    { q: "A comparable has beta_E = 1.60, D/E = 0.5 and beta_D = 0.20. What is beta_U?", correct: "1.1333", distractors: ["1.0667", "0.9000", "1.1511"], why: "beta_U = (E/V)beta_E + (D/V)beta_D with E/V = 2/3 and D/V = 1/3: (2/3)(1.60) + (1/3)(0.20) = 1.1333. Weighting with D/E instead of D/V, dropping the debt term, or reaching for the tax-adjusted Hamada formula each gives one of the wrong answers.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "beta_U = 1.10, your target D/E = 0.6 and beta_D = 0.15. What is beta_E?", correct: "1.6700", distractors: ["1.7600", "1.6148", "1.1900"], why: "beta_E = beta_U + (D/E)(beta_U - beta_D) = 1.10 + 0.6(1.10 - 0.15) = 1.67. Forgetting to subtract beta_D gives 1.76; the tax-adjusted version gives 1.6148.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "A comparable has rE = 15%, rD = 6% and D/E = 0.5. What is rU?", correct: "12.0000 %", distractors: ["10.5000 %", "10.0000 %", "11.5600 %"], why: "Pre-tax WACC = rU: (2/3)(15) + (1/3)(6) = 12%. The trap is the simple average of 10.5%, which uses D/E = 0.5 as if it were D/V = 0.5.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "rU = 10%, rD = 5% and D/E = 1. What is rE?", correct: "15.0000 %", distractors: ["12.5000 %", "20.0000 %", "5.0000 %"], why: "MM II: rE = rU + (D/E)(rU - rD) = 10 + 1(10 - 5) = 15%. Using D/V = 0.5 gives 12.5%, forgetting to subtract rD gives 20%, and flipping the sign inside the bracket gives 5%.", ch: 6 },
    { q: "rU = 12%, rD = 4% and D/E = 0.4. What is rE?", correct: "15.2000 %", distractors: ["13.6000 %", "18.4000 %", "16.8000 %"], why: "rE = 12 + 0.4(12 - 4) = 15.2%. The 13.6% answer adds (D/E)rD instead of (D/E)(rU - rD), which is the most common slip on this formula.", ch: 6 },
    { q: "rE = 14%, rD = 7%, D/V = 0.40 and tau_c = 22%. What is the after-tax WACC?", correct: "10.5840 %", distractors: ["11.2000 %", "8.7360 %", "11.5600 %"], why: "rwacc = (E/V)rE + (D/V)rD(1 - tau_c) = 0.6(14) + 0.4(7)(0.78) = 10.584%. The tax factor applies to the debt term only; applying it to the whole average gives 8.736%.", ch: 17 },
    { q: "rE = 16%, rD = 8% and E/V = 0.75. What is rU?", correct: "14.0000 %", distractors: ["12.0000 %", "13.5600 %", "16.0000 %"], why: "Pre-tax WACC equals rU: 0.75(16) + 0.25(8) = 14%. Slipping the tax factor into this step gives 13.56% and would corrupt every later number, because rU must be a pre-tax quantity.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "rf = 3%, beta_E = 1.4 and the market risk premium is 5%. What is rE?", correct: "10.0000 %", distractors: ["7.0000 %", "9.2000 %", "8.0000 %"], why: "CAPM: rE = rf + beta_E x MRP = 3 + 1.4(5) = 10%. Note the premium is already a premium here; do not subtract rf from it a second time.", ch: 3 },
    { q: "rf = 2%, beta = 0.8 and the expected market return is 8%. What is the required return?", correct: "6.8000 %", distractors: ["8.4000 %", "6.4000 %", "4.8000 %"], why: "Here 8% is the market return, not the premium, so the premium is 8 - 2 = 6: r = 2 + 0.8(6) = 6.8%. Multiplying beta by 8% instead gives 8.4%, the classic misread.", ch: 3 },
    { q: "rf = 3%, beta_D = 0.25 and the market risk premium is 6%. What is rD?", correct: "4.5000 %", distractors: ["1.5000 %", "9.0000 %", "3.7500 %"], why: "Debt is priced by the CAPM too: rD = 3 + 0.25(6) = 4.5%. A non-zero debt beta must then be carried into beta_U = (E/V)beta_E + (D/V)beta_D.", ch: 5 },
    { q: "A bond promises a yield of 8%. The default probability is 4% and the loss given default is 60% of face value. What is the expected return rD?", correct: "5.6000 %", distractors: ["8.0000 %", "7.9600 %", "7.6800 %"], why: "rD = y - p·L = 8 - 0.04(60) = 5.6%. The promised yield overstates the cost of debt, so using 8% in a WACC would understate the firm's value.", ch: 5 },
    { q: "rU = 11%, D/V = 0.30, rD = 6% and tau_c = 22%. What is the after-tax WACC?", correct: "10.6040 %", distractors: ["9.6800 %", "11.0000 %", "10.9340 %"], why: "rwacc = rU - d·tau_c·rD = 11 - 0.30(0.22)(6) = 10.604%. This shortcut avoids relevering rE altogether and is a fast consistency check on the long route.", ch: 17 },
    { q: "tau_c = 22% and the firm has permanent debt of 500. What is PV(TS)?", correct: "110", distractors: ["390", "500", "55"], why: "With permanent debt the shield is tau_c·rD·D discounted at rD, which collapses to tau_c·D = 0.22(500) = 110. Note that rD cancels: the value of the shield does not depend on the interest rate.", ch: 7 },
    { q: "tau_c = 25% and the firm has permanent debt of 800. What is PV(TS)?", correct: "200", distractors: ["600", "800", "100"], why: "tau_c·D = 0.25(800) = 200. The complement 600 is the after-tax value of the debt principal, not the shield.", ch: 7 },
    { q: "V^U = 1000, tau_c = 22% and the firm takes on permanent debt of 400. What is V^L?", correct: "1088", distractors: ["1400", "912", "1000"], why: "V^L = V^U + tau_c·D = 1000 + 88 = 1088. Only the tax saving is added, never the debt principal itself, since the debt is money the firm has to pay back.", ch: 7 },
    { q: "Debt of 1000 at rD = 5% is outstanding for one year only, tau_c = 22%. What is the tax shield worth today?", correct: "10.4762", distractors: ["11.0000", "220.0000", "209.5238"], why: "The shield is tau_c·rD·D = 0.22(0.05)(1000) = 11, received in one year and discounted at rD: 11/1.05 = 10.4762. It is 11, not 220: the deduction is on the interest, not on the principal.", ch: 7 },
    { q: "Permanent debt of 500 at rD = 6%, tau_c = 22%. Compute PV(TS) from the stream of shields.", correct: "110.0000", distractors: ["6.6000", "30.0000", "500.0000"], why: "Interest is 0.06(500) = 30, the annual shield is 0.22(30) = 6.6, and a perpetuity discounted at rD gives 6.6/0.06 = 110 = tau_c·D. Seeing the two routes agree is the point of the exercise.", ch: 18 },
    { q: "Next year's tax shield is 0.25 x 0.05 x 2000 = 25 and it grows 2% forever. The firm keeps a constant D/E and rU = 10%. What is PV(TS)?", correct: "312.5000", distractors: ["250.0000", "500.0000", "833.3333"], why: "Constant D/E means the shield is discounted at rU: 25/(0.10 - 0.02) = 312.50. Discounting at rD = 5% instead gives 833.33, which is the single most common error on this item.", ch: 18 },
    { q: "An all-equity firm is worth 800 with 100 shares. It announces permanent debt of 300, tau_c = 22%. What is the share price right after the announcement?", correct: "8.6600", distractors: ["8.0000", "11.0000", "7.3400"], why: "The market capitalises tau_c·D = 66 immediately, so the price goes from 8.00 to (800 + 66)/100 = 8.66 before any share is repurchased. That is how the existing shareholders capture the gain.", ch: 7 },
    { q: "A firm is worth 600 with 100 shares and no debt. In a perfect market it borrows 180 to repurchase shares. How many shares are retired?", correct: "30", distractors: ["18", "36", "25"], why: "Shares retired = D/P = 180/6 = 30, at the unchanged price of 6 per share. In a perfect market there is no announcement effect, so the pre- and post-announcement prices are the same.", ch: 6 },
    { q: "Same recapitalisation: after borrowing 180 and retiring 30 shares, what is the price per share?", correct: "6.00", distractors: ["4.20", "8.57", "5.00"], why: "Equity is worth 600 - 180 = 420 across 70 shares, so the price is 6.00, exactly as before. Dividing 420 by the original 100 shares (4.20) forgets that shares were retired.", ch: 6 },
    { q: "After the recapitalisation D = 180 and E = 420. With rU = 10% and rD = 5%, what is rE?", correct: "12.1429 %", distractors: ["11.5000 %", "21.6667 %", "10.0000 %"], why: "D/E = 180/420 = 0.4286, so rE = 10 + 0.4286(10 - 5) = 12.1429%. Check: the pre-tax WACC (420/600)(12.1429) + (180/600)(5) returns 10% = rU.", ch: 6 },
    { q: "A share trades at 40 cum-dividend and pays a dividend of 2. What is the ex-dividend price in a perfect market?", correct: "38.00", distractors: ["40.00", "42.00", "39.00"], why: "The price falls by the full dividend, since the buyer no longer receives it. The shareholder's total wealth is unchanged: 38 in shares plus 2 in cash.", ch: 15 },
    { q: "A firm worth 1000 with 100 shares repurchases 200 of stock. What is the price afterwards?", correct: "10.00", distractors: ["8.00", "12.50", "9.00"], why: "It retires 20 shares, leaving 800 of value across 80 shares: still 10.00. A repurchase does not move the price in a perfect market, which is exactly why it is equivalent to a dividend.", ch: 15 },
    { q: "The same firm pays out 200 as a dividend instead. What is the price afterwards?", correct: "8.00", distractors: ["10.00", "12.50", "6.00"], why: "800 of value across the unchanged 100 shares gives 8.00, plus 2 per share in cash. Same total wealth as under the repurchase, which is payout irrelevance in two lines.", ch: 15 },
    { q: "Cash flow will be 120 or 40 with equal probability and the debt has face value 60. What is the equity worth (ignore discounting)?", correct: "30.00", distractors: ["20.00", "60.00", "80.00"], why: "Equity = E[max(CF - 60, 0)] = 0.5(60) + 0.5(0) = 30. Computing E[CF] - 60 = 20 instead ignores limited liability, which is the whole source of the agency problems.", ch: 9 },
    { q: "Cash flow will be 100 or 40 with equal probability and the debt has face value 80. What is the debt worth (ignore discounting)?", correct: "60.00", distractors: ["80.00", "70.00", "40.00"], why: "Debt = E[min(CF, 80)] = 0.5(80) + 0.5(40) = 60. The 20 shortfall against the promised 80 is the value of the put the creditors have written.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "Firm value will be 50 or 150 with equal probability; debt face value is 100. Shareholders can invest 20 of their own money to raise value by 30 in both states. What is the change in equity value, net of the investment?", correct: "-5.00", distractors: ["+10.00", "+15.00", "+30.00"], why: "Equity goes from 0.5(0) + 0.5(50) = 25 to 0.5(0) + 0.5(80) = 40, but costs 20, so 40 - 20 - 25 = -5. Shareholders decline even though the project's NPV is +10: this is debt overhang.", ch: 10 },
    { q: "Same setup: firm value 50 or 150, face value 100, an investment of 20 that adds 30 in both states. How much do the creditors gain?", correct: "15.00", distractors: ["10.00", "0.00", "30.00"], why: "Debt goes from 0.5(50) + 0.5(100) = 75 to 0.5(80) + 0.5(100) = 90. The check: creditors +15 and shareholders -5 sum to +10, exactly the project's NPV.", ch: 10 },
    { q: "The safe project makes the firm worth 100 for sure; the risky one gives 30 or 150 with equal probability. Face value of debt is 90. What is equity worth under the risky project?", correct: "30.00", distractors: ["10.00", "0.00", "60.00"], why: "0.5(150 - 90) + 0.5(0) = 30, against only 100 - 90 = 10 under the safe project. Shareholders pick the risky project although it destroys 10 of firm value: asset substitution.", ch: 9 },
    { q: "Same two projects (safe 100 for sure, risky 30 or 150, face value 90). What is the agency cost of debt?", correct: "10.00", distractors: ["20.00", "30.00", "0.00"], why: "First-best value 100 minus the value actually chosen 90 = 10. Always quantify it as a value difference, not as the transfer between the claimholders.", ch: 9 },
    { q: "The safe project pays 100 for sure; the risky one pays 40 or 160 with equal probability. At which face value of debt are shareholders indifferent between them?", correct: "40", distractors: ["60", "80", "100"], why: "Set 100 - K = 0.5(160 - K): 100 - K = 80 - 0.5K gives K = 40. Above that face value the risky project wins, which is the answer to the recurring find-the-debt-level sub-question.", ch: 9 },
    { q: "S = 50, K = 50, the one-year European put is worth 5 and rf = 5%. What is the call worth?", correct: "7.3810", distractors: ["5.0000", "2.6190", "7.5000"], why: "Parity: C = P + S - PV(K) = 5 + 50 - 50/1.05 = 7.3810. The call exceeds the put here purely because the strike is paid later.", ch: 21 },
    { q: "S = 100, K = 100, the one-year European call is worth 8 and rf = 4%. What is the put worth?", correct: "4.1538", distractors: ["8.0000", "11.8462", "12.0000"], why: "Rearranged parity: P = C - S + PV(K) = 8 - 100 + 96.1538 = 4.1538. Getting the sign of S and PV(K) the wrong way round gives 11.8462.", ch: 21 },
    { q: "S0 = 100 and the price moves to 120 or 90 in one period; rf = 5%. What is the risk-neutral probability of the up move?", correct: "0.5000", distractors: ["0.3333", "0.6667", "0.4762"], why: "rho = ((1.05)(100) - 90)/(120 - 90) = 15/30 = 0.5. Forgetting to grow S0 by (1 + rf) gives 0.3333, the most frequent slip in the binomial questions.", ch: 22 },
    { q: "S0 = 50 and the price moves to 60 or 40 in one period; rf = 4%. What is the risk-neutral probability of the up move?", correct: "0.6000", distractors: ["0.5000", "0.4000", "0.5769"], why: "rho = (52 - 40)/(60 - 40) = 0.6. It is a probability, so it is not discounted; dividing it by 1.04 gives the spurious 0.5769.", ch: 22 },
    { q: "S0 = 100 moving to 120 or 90, rf = 5%, K = 100. What is the one-period call worth?", correct: "9.5238", distractors: ["10.0000", "9.0909", "20.0000"], why: "C = (rho·Cu + (1 - rho)Cd)/(1 + rf) = (0.5 x 20)/1.05 = 9.5238. Skipping the discounting gives 10, and the real probabilities never enter anywhere.", ch: 22 },
    { q: "Same tree (100 to 120 or 90, K = 100). What is the replicating share position Delta?", correct: "0.6667", distractors: ["0.5000", "1.0000", "0.3333"], why: "Delta = (Cu - Cd)/(Su - Sd) = 20/30 = 0.6667. It is the ratio of payoff spreads, not a probability, and it never equals rho except by coincidence.", ch: 22 },
    { q: "Same tree with Delta = 2/3. What is the bond position B in the replicating portfolio?", correct: "-57.1429", distractors: ["-60.0000", "57.1429", "-66.6667"], why: "B = (Cd - Sd·Delta)/(1 + rf) = (0 - 60)/1.05 = -57.1429, a borrowing. Check: C = S·Delta + B = 66.6667 - 57.1429 = 9.5238, matching the risk-neutral answer.", ch: 22 },
    { q: "A firm is worth 100 today and will be worth 150 or 70 in a year; rf = 5% and the debt has face value 80. What is the risk-neutral probability of the up state?", correct: "0.4375", distractors: ["0.5000", "0.3750", "0.4167"], why: "rho = (1.05 x 100 - 70)/(150 - 70) = 35/80 = 0.4375. The underlying here is the whole firm, not the share price, which is what makes equity a call on it.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "Same firm (100 today, 150 or 70, rf = 5%, face value 80). What is the equity worth today?", correct: "29.1667", distractors: ["30.6250", "33.3333", "35.0000"], why: "Equity is a call: payoffs 70 and 0, so E = 0.4375(70)/1.05 = 29.1667. Using the real probability of 0.5 gives 33.3333 and is simply wrong, however tempting the symmetry looks.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "Same firm. What is the debt worth today?", correct: "70.8333", distractors: ["76.1905", "80.0000", "66.6667"], why: "D = V - E = 100 - 29.1667 = 70.8333. The risk-free value 80/1.05 = 76.1905 minus the written put gives the same number, and the difference is the value of the default option.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "The debt is worth 70.8333 today and promises 80 in one year. What is the promised yield?", correct: "12.9412 %", distractors: ["11.4583 %", "5.0000 %", "13.0000 %"], why: "y = 80/70.8333 - 1 = 12.9412%. Computing the gain as a fraction of face value instead of price gives 11.4583%, a standard yield-versus-discount confusion.", ch: 24 },
    { q: "The promised yield is 12.9412% and rf = 5%. What is the credit spread?", correct: "7.9412 %", distractors: ["12.9412 %", "2.5882 %", "17.9412 %"], why: "The spread is y - rf = 7.9412 percentage points. Only what remains after subtracting expected default losses, p·L, is a genuine risk premium; the rest just pays for defaults.", ch: 5 },
    { q: "A project's NPV today is 10. In one year it will be 40 or -20 with equal probability; r = 10%. What is the option to wait worth?", correct: "8.1818", distractors: ["18.1818", "9.0909", "10.0000"], why: "Waiting is worth (0.5 x 40 + 0.5 x 0)/1.1 = 18.1818, and you give up today's NPV of 10, so the option is worth 8.1818. Since it is positive, you wait even though the NPV today is already positive.", ch: 25 },
    { q: "A project's NPV is 60 with probability 0.5 and -20 with probability 0.5. What is perfect information worth today, before any discounting?", correct: "10.00", distractors: ["30.00", "20.00", "40.00"], why: "E[max(0, NPV)] - max(0, E[NPV]) = 30 - 20 = 10. Skipping the second term reports 30 and forgets that you would have invested anyway on the strength of the average.", ch: 25 },
    { q: "A failing project loses 10 per year forever and r = 10%. If you can walk away after three years, what is that exit option worth?", correct: "75.1315", distractors: ["100.0000", "24.8685", "70.0000"], why: "Committed forever it costs 10/0.10 = 100; truncated to a three-year annuity it costs 10 x 2.4869 = 24.8685. The option is the difference, 75.1315, which is a perpetuity replaced by an annuity.", ch: 25 },
    { q: "Continuing a project yields 8 per year forever at r = 10%. At what salvage value are you indifferent between continuing and exiting?", correct: "80.00", distractors: ["8.00", "0.80", "88.00"], why: "You exit when the salvage value exceeds the present value of continuing, 8/0.10 = 80. Solving for the indifference point like this is the standard closing sub-question of the real-options exercise.", ch: 25 },
    { q: "A firm just generated a cash flow of 100. It grows 3% forever and the discount rate is 8%. What is it worth today?", correct: "2060.00", distractors: ["2000.00", "1250.00", "3433.33"], why: "The growing perpetuity formula needs next year's cash flow: 100(1.03)/(0.08 - 0.03) = 2060. Using 100 instead of 100 x 1.03 gives 2000 and is an error the solution keys call out by name.", ch: 1 },
    { q: "What is a perpetuity of 15 per year worth when r = 12%?", correct: "125.0000", distractors: ["180.0000", "12.5000", "1.8000"], why: "PV = C/r = 15/0.12 = 125. The formula gives the value one period before the first payment, which matters when the exam starts the stream in year 3.", ch: 1 },
    { q: "Free cash flow next year is 50, the WACC is 9% and growth is 3% forever. What is the firm worth?", correct: "833.3333", distractors: ["555.5556", "416.6667", "1666.6667"], why: "V^L = FCF1/(rwacc - g) = 50/0.06 = 833.33. Forgetting the growth in the denominator gives 555.56; the sensitivity to g is why the exam likes to solve backwards for it.", ch: 17 },
    { q: "EBIT is 200, tau_c = 22%, depreciation 50, CapEx 70 and the change in NWC is +10. What is free cash flow?", correct: "126.00", distractors: ["170.00", "26.00", "136.00"], why: "FCF = 200(0.78) + 50 - 70 - 10 = 126. Depreciation is added back because it is not cash, and interest never appears anywhere in this line.", ch: 2 },
    { q: "Net working capital is 10% of sales and sales rise from 500 to 600. What is the effect on this year's free cash flow?", correct: "-10.00", distractors: ["-60.00", "+10.00", "-100.00"], why: "NWC rises from 50 to 60, so the change of +10 is subtracted. Only the change is a cash flow; subtracting the level of 60 is a common and expensive slip.", ch: 2 },
    { q: "Free cash flow is 50 in year 1 and 60 in year 2, and the WACC is 10%. What is the value today?", correct: "95.0413", distractors: ["100.0000", "110.0000", "90.9091"], why: "50/1.10 + 60/1.10^2 = 45.4545 + 49.5868 = 95.0413. Discounting year 2 only once gives 100, and not discounting at all gives 110.", ch: 17 },
    { q: "The firm keeps d = D/V = 0.30 and the levered continuation value at the end of year 1 is 850. What is D_1?", correct: "255.00", distractors: ["595.00", "850.00", "300.00"], why: "D_1 = d·V_1^L = 0.30(850) = 255. If the previous debt level was higher, the difference is repaid that year, and next year's tax shield is computed on 255. This was H2025 Exercise 4(f).", ch: 19 },
    { q: "Free cash flow to equity is 30 per year forever and rE = 15%. What is the equity worth?", correct: "200.00", distractors: ["250.00", "300.00", "4.50"], why: "FTE discounts the equity cash flow at the equity rate: 30/0.15 = 200. Discounting it at the WACC (12%) or at rU (10%) mixes a levered cash flow with an unlevered rate.", ch: 19 },
    { q: "tau_c = 30%, the personal tax rate on equity income is 20% and on interest income 35%. What is the effective tax advantage of debt?", correct: "0.1385", distractors: ["0.3000", "0.4400", "0.1500"], why: "tau* = 1 - (1 - 0.30)(1 - 0.20)/(1 - 0.35) = 1 - 0.8615 = 0.1385. Personal taxes shrink the corporate advantage from 30% to under 14%, and can wipe it out entirely.", ch: 8 },
    { q: "The firm's target capital structure is D/V = 0.40. What is D/E?", correct: "0.6667", distractors: ["0.4000", "1.5000", "2.5000"], why: "D/E = (D/V)/(1 - D/V) = 0.4/0.6 = 0.6667. MM II needs D/E while the WACC needs D/V, and confusing the two is the fastest way to lose a chain of sub-questions.", ch: 4 },
    { q: "Acquirer: 100 shares at 20. Target: 50 shares at 10. Synergies 200. The exchange ratio is 0.6. What is the post-merger share price?", correct: "20.7692", distractors: ["20.0000", "19.2308", "27.0000"], why: "New shares x = 0.6(50) = 30, so P_new = (2000 + 500 + 200)/130 = 20.7692. Leaving the synergies out gives 19.2308; forgetting the new shares gives 27.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "Same deal (P_new = 20.7692, 100 acquirer shares). How much do the acquirer's shareholders gain?", correct: "76.9231", distractors: ["200.0000", "123.0769", "0.0000"], why: "100(20.7692) - 2000 = 76.92, and the target's shareholders take the remaining 123.08. The two gains must add to the synergy of 200, which is the check to run.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "Same two firms (A = 2000 over 100 shares, T = 500 over 50 shares, S = 200). What is the maximum exchange ratio the acquirer can offer?", correct: "0.7000", distractors: ["0.6000", "1.0000", "0.5000"], why: "At the bound the target receives T + S = 700, so 2700x/(100 + x) = 700 gives x = 35 and ER = 35/50 = 0.70. At that ratio P_new = 20, exactly the acquirer's pre-deal price.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "The exchange ratio is 0.6, the acquirer traded at 20, the target at 10, and P_new = 20.7692. What premium do the target's shareholders actually receive?", correct: "24.62 %", distractors: ["20.00 %", "3.85 %", "2.46 %"], why: "They receive 0.6(20.7692) = 12.4615 per target share, which is 24.62% above 10. The offered premium computed at the old acquirer price is only 20%: the difference is the synergy being reflected in P_new.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "The acquirer earns 100 with 100 shares; the target earns 20. The acquirer issues 30 new shares to buy it. What is the post-merger EPS?", correct: "0.9231", distractors: ["1.0000", "1.2000", "0.8889"], why: "EPS = 120/130 = 0.9231, down from 1.00, so this deal is dilutive. Accretion or dilution reflects the relative P/E ratios, not whether the deal creates value.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "A target traded at 30 before the bid, the offer is worth 60, and the target now trades at 45. What probability does the market attach to the deal closing?", correct: "0.5000", distractors: ["0.3333", "0.7500", "0.2500"], why: "45 = p(60) + (1 - p)(30) gives p = (45 - 30)/(60 - 30) = 0.5. Always write the two outcomes down before solving; that framing was worth most of the points in H2025 Exercise 6.", ch: 26 },
    { q: "An IPO is priced at 20, closes its first day at 26, and 5 million shares were sold. How much money was left on the table?", correct: "30 million", distractors: ["130 million", "100 million", "6 million"], why: "5 million x (26 - 20) = 30 million, a 30% underpricing. It is a real cost to the pre-IPO owners, who sold their shares 6 below the price the market immediately paid.", ch: 14 },
    { q: "A firm sells 10 million shares at an offer price of 20 with a 7% underwriting spread. What are the net proceeds?", correct: "186 million", distractors: ["200 million", "193 million", "14 million"], why: "Net per share is 20(0.93) = 18.60, so 186 million reaches the firm and 14 million goes to the syndicate. The underpricing cost comes on top of this spread.", ch: 14 },
    { q: "A firm must raise I = 60. Under investors' beliefs the post-issue firm is worth 300. What fraction alpha must the new investors receive?", correct: "0.2000", distractors: ["0.2500", "0.1500", "0.8000"], why: "alpha = I/(post-issue value under investors' beliefs) = 60/300 = 0.20. The price is set on beliefs, not on the truth, which is the entire source of the Myers-Majluf distortion.", ch: 12 },
    { q: "The issue gives new investors alpha = 0.20 and the true post-issue value is 260, against 160 if the firm does nothing. What is the old shareholders' payoff?", correct: "208.00", distractors: ["260.00", "240.00", "200.00"], why: "(1 - alpha) x 260 = 208, which beats 160, so this type issues. The final step is to check that the beliefs producing alpha are rational given who chooses to issue.", ch: 12 },
    { q: "You relevered beta_U = 1.10 to beta_E = 1.67 at D/E = 0.6 with beta_D = 0.15. What does the check (E/V)beta_E + (D/V)beta_D give?", correct: "1.1000", distractors: ["0.9100", "1.6700", "1.8200"], why: "(1/1.6)(1.67) + (0.6/1.6)(0.15) = 1.0438 + 0.0563 = 1.10, back to beta_U. This is one of the five consistency checks the examiner performs, and it costs ten seconds.", ch: 4 },
  ],
};
